// weave_spec.md §1: two comment forms. `//` runs to end of line (as
// used throughout every other example); `/* ... */` is the newer
// addition demonstrated here — doesn't nest (same as Go), can sit
// mid-line, and never itself acts as a statement terminator even when
// it spans several physical lines.

/* A block comment can sit at top level, on its own line(s),
   before any code at all. */

main = fn(args) {
	x = /* mid-line */ 1 + 2
	print(x)

	/* A block comment that spans multiple lines is pure whitespace —
	   it does NOT break the statement it sits inside of, unlike an
	   actual newline would. */
	y = 1 /* line one
	         line two
	         line three */ + 1
	print(y)

	// The two forms nest into each other just fine, since only "/*"
	// itself starts a block comment — "//" has no special meaning once
	// already inside one.
	/* a block comment containing // is still just one block comment */
	print("done")

	return 0
}
